From a1ec3eefd219d7936da4a321f0ff9edacb594b2e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CreateCVOnline <dev@createcvonline.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 16:39:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Add the language switcher and carry the site language
 into the app
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

"In the user dashboard I can't see where I change the language" — there was
nowhere. The marketing header has had a language control since the French
release, and the dashboard renders no marketing header, so signing in
removed the only way to change language.

The switcher now sits in the dashboard header (so it is findable) and in
Settings (so it reads as a saved account preference). Both write to
/api/account/locale, which updates the profile and the cookie together.

`profile.locale` has existed since the first commit and nothing ever
rendered from it. It is now the interface language, resolved once in the
dashboard layout — on the server, so pages do not paint in English and then
flip — and handed down through a provider. `lib/i18n/app-copy.ts` holds the
strings; a missing translation is a type error rather than a silent English
fallback, which is the exact failure that left the German footer in English.

A cookie carries the language across the sign-up boundary: someone who reads
the French pages and clicks "Créer mon CV" has no profile yet, so /register
has nothing to read. Their first profile adopts it, and a returning user's
saved preference is never overwritten.

Two bugs in that cookie, both found in a browser and neither reproducible
with curl:

- `localeOf('/dashboard')` is 'en', because the dashboard has no locale
  prefix — so writing it unconditionally reset a French user's language to
  English every time they opened their own dashboard. Only paths belonging
  to a translated cluster may write it.
- Next.js prefetches every link in the viewport, and those prefetches reach
  the proxy like any other request, so a French page linking to an English
  one rewrote the language before the user clicked anything. Guarded on
  `sec-fetch-dest: document`.

The dashboard shell, its navigation and Settings are translated. The
remaining dashboard pages, the auth pages and the editor still read from
English literals; the catalogue they need is written and waiting.

1,643 tests; SEO check 268 pages, 0 errors.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KqU6xXaZUtNkv28VRtkast
